elseif($page==='help'){
 heading('WORKING SAFELY','Kitchen guide','A practical guide to using your temperature-monitoring records.');
 echo '<section class="card prose"><h2>Before the first shift</h2><ol><li>Administrator: create kitchens and protected manager accounts. Staff use the kitchen frontend without signing in.</li><li>Manager: add each fridge, freezer and hot-holding unit, plus opening, closing or cleaning checks. Set check times and operating days.</li><li>Review and validate limits, cooling processes, measurement methods and monitoring frequency against your written HACCP plan. Record the control review.</li><li>Train staff to measure temperatures correctly, sanitise probes, act on problems and report missing checks honestly.</li></ol><h2>During a shift</h2><p>Select the kitchen, enter your name or initials, choose the check type and record the actual measured value and time. Names entered on the frontend are self-reported; manager actions require sign-in. Select a scheduled slot when completing a planned check. Extra readings remain extra readings. A reading over 15 minutes old needs an explanation. A scheduled check over 30 minutes late also needs a reason. A failed check requires immediate action notes.</p><p>Cooking and reheating require continuous holding time as well as temperature. Cooling is a two-stage record: start when cooking ends, then save the actual finish time, temperature and chilled-storage location. An unfinished cooling record becomes overdue when its target time passes. Dashboard counts update when the page is refreshed; there are no automatic sensor readings or background notifications.</p><h2>When something goes wrong</h2><p>Protect the food and follow your approved corrective procedure. Record action and follow-up measurements against the original check. Managers must record the final disposition. A passing follow-up does not erase the original failure. Corrections are explanatory additions; they cannot silently replace a reading. A manager may document disposal, rejection or another justified outcome.</p><h2>Rice needs particular care</h2><p>Cool promptly, ideally within one hour, then refrigerate. Use refrigerated cooked rice within 24 hours and reheat only once. The portal starts rice cooling with a configurable house target of ≤8°C within 60 minutes. This is a stricter numeric house control, not a quotation of a universal FSA cooling limit. Review the actual process for your batch sizes and equipment.</p><h2>Starting limits and assumptions</h2><ul><li>Fridges and chilled deliveries: operating target ≤5°C; readings above 5°C need attention and above 8°C are critical. Product instructions may require colder conditions.</li><li>Freezers and frozen deliveries: example operating target ≤−18°C. Check the food and supplier specification as well.</li><li>Hot holding: ≥63°C. The portal flags all readings below this threshold; it does not automatically apply time exceptions.</li><li>Cooking / reheating: house standard ≥75°C continuously for at least 30 seconds. Other validated combinations exist. Review Scotland’s specific reheating requirements before use there.</li><li>General cooling: example house target ≤8°C within 90 minutes. This requires validation for your process; it is not a universal legal limit.</li><li>Probe checks: example ice-point tolerance −1 to +1°C; boiling-point tolerance 99 to 101°C at normal atmospheric pressure. Follow manufacturer instructions and account for boiling-point variation.</li></ul><h2>Review and evidence</h2><p>Managers should review missed slots and exceptions daily. A daily review is a snapshot of what was known at the time. Schedule revisions preserve earlier slots. Download CSV for a machine-readable record including events, reviews and schedule evidence. Use Print / save as PDF for a readable inspection report. Reports use measured dates; later follow-up events are included with their own timestamps.</p><h2>Scope and responsibility</h2><p>This portal supports monitoring and corrective-action evidence. It does not replace a food-business-specific HACCP plan, staff training, hazard analysis, supplier controls, allergen management or competent food-safety advice. It is manually entered and requires an internet/server connection. It does not certify food safety or guarantee a hygiene rating. Application records are append-only through the portal; a server/database administrator can still modify the database. Maintain independent backups.</p><h2>Official sources</h2><p>Starting guidance checked 22 October 2026. Review it periodically and when your menu, processes or location change.</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/food-hygiene-businesses/cooking" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GOV.UK — Cooking, reheating and hot holding</a></li><li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/food-hygiene-businesses/chilling-and-freezing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GOV.UK — Chilling and freezing</a></li><li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/food-safety-management-systems" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GOV.UK — Food safety management systems</a></li><li><a href="https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/home-food-fact-checker" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Food Standards Agency — Rice storage and reheating</a></li><li><a href="https://www.foodstandards.gov.scot/business-guidance/running-a-food-business/publications/cooksafe-guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Food Standards Scotland — CookSafe</a></li><li><a href="https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/safer-food-better-business-sfbb" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Food Standards Agency — Safer food, better business (including probe checks)</a></li></ul></section>';
}
